5:[["$","$L10","1.9",{"topic":{"id":"1.9","title":"Information Technology Rules, 2011","minutes":13,"summary":"Understand the SPDI Rules: notice, consent, purpose limitation, disclosure, security, and the limits of a compliance checklist.","sections":[{"title":"Scope and sensitive personal data","body":["The Information Technology (Reasonable Security Practices and Procedures and Sensitive Personal Data or Information) Rules, 2011 were framed under section 43A read with section 87(2)(ob). They apply to body corporates handling covered data, with definitions and qualifications in the Rules. Rule 3 covers specified categories such as passwords, financial account information, health information, sexual orientation, biometric information, and certain records received for processing.","Information freely available in the public domain or furnished under the Right to Information Act is excluded from the SPDI definition. That exclusion does not mean all public information may be used for any purpose without legal or ethical consequences. The separate 2011 Intermediaries Due Diligence Rules should not be confused with the SPDI Rules merely because both share a year."]},{"title":"Collection and disclosure duties","body":["Rule 5 addresses collection, notice, consent, lawful purpose, retention, review, withdrawal, and grievance contact. Sensitive-data collection requires consent in writing through letter, fax, or email under the Rule’s wording. The provider must use information for the purpose collected and retain it only as required or necessary under the stated framework. Consent is not the only legal concept relevant to an investigation.","Rule 6 regulates disclosure to third parties, subject to permission and specified exceptions including legally mandated government requests. Rule 7 addresses secure transfer. A compliance officer cannot simply approve a broad disclosure because a requester says they are investigating fraud; identity, authority, scope, and documentation still matter."]},{"title":"Security practices and the current context","body":["Rule 8 addresses reasonable security practices, including documented policies, managerial and technical controls, and specified standards or audited practices. A named standard is not a guarantee against every breach. Investigators should examine actual access controls, risk assessment, monitoring, incident handling, and whether the implemented policy matched the system in question.","The DPDP Act, 2023 provides a separate personal-data regime, including a repeal pathway for section 43A when the relevant commencement notification is issued. Check current commencement and transitional material rather than assuming the 2011 Rules vanished when the Act was passed. For historical incidents, the version in force at the relevant time may be crucial."]}],"practice":{"title":"Audit a sign-up form","scenario":"A wellness app collects health details and a password but provides no purpose notice or grievance contact.","steps":["Classify the data under Rule 3 using the actual fields.","Compare the collection flow against Rule 5.","Identify the required consent, notice, and contact information.","Review security and retention rather than treating consent as the entire compliance exercise."],"debrief":"A privacy review connects the user interface to data flows, contracts, security controls, and retention. A checkbox alone cannot cure unrelated deficiencies."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-2011","dpdp","it-act"],"sources":["rules","itact","dpdp","privacy"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain scope and sensitive personal data and apply it to an investigation.","Explain collection and disclosure duties and apply it to an investigation.","Explain security practices and the current context and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.9","prompt":"What is the principal subject of the SPDI Rules, 2011?","options":["Digital signature certificate issuance only.","Reasonable security practices and sensitive personal data handling.","The replacement of the IPC.","International cyber warfare strategy."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SPDI Rules implement the section 43A framework for covered body corporates and sensitive personal data.","unit":1}}}]]
